WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:07.379
Pat Mitchell: 11 years ago,
you, your sister --

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.880
Glenn Close: 14 years ago.

00:00:08.922 --> 00:00:11.132
PM: 14 years ago!

00:00:11.174 --> 00:00:16.846
Your sister, your nephew and yourself
made this courageous decision

00:00:16.888 --> 00:00:19.015
to step forward and say,

00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:21.726
"Our family has suffered
from mental illness,"

00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:25.605
at a time when very few people
were talking publicly.

00:00:25.605 --> 00:00:29.985
What led to this decision for the family?

00:00:29.985 --> 00:00:35.282
GC: My sister Jessie, my younger sister,
always the wild one --

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:37.325
my dad, who was a doctor, actually said,

00:00:37.325 --> 00:00:40.745
"Pull up your socks,
get back to work, get an education."

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:45.500
She came up to me one summer,
having put her kids in the car,

00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:47.711
and she said, "I need your help.

00:00:47.752 --> 00:00:50.463
I can't stop thinking
about killing myself."

00:00:52.465 --> 00:00:54.175
I was in shock.

00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:58.013
I had absolutely no clue,

00:00:58.054 --> 00:01:01.600
no clue of what she was dealing with.

00:01:01.641 --> 00:01:05.145
So my mom and I were able to get her help.

00:01:06.104 --> 00:01:11.318
Her son Calen suffers from schizophrenia.

00:01:13.111 --> 00:01:15.030
Lives with schizophrenia.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.325
And together they came to me and said,

00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:23.246
"We have -- we're trying to learn
how to manage our illnesses,"

00:01:23.246 --> 00:01:27.876
which in long-term recovery means
you have to change meds.

00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:30.378
It's how many meds? It's what --

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.758
It's a process and it's a long process,
and you have to be very resilient.

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:37.969
But they said, "We have found

00:01:38.011 --> 00:01:43.266
that the stigma
around what we are dealing with

00:01:43.308 --> 00:01:46.936
can be worse than
the diseases themselves."

00:01:46.978 --> 00:01:51.483
When Calen came back
from two years in a psychiatric hospital,

00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:53.735
none of his friends came back.

00:01:55.528 --> 00:01:59.616
They still haven't come back,
even though he's become this advocate.

00:01:59.616 --> 00:02:01.451
It's incredible to me.

00:02:01.451 --> 00:02:05.121
So I said, because they're my family,

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:07.290
I said, "Of course, I will help you,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.378
but you have to do it with me

00:02:11.419 --> 00:02:16.049
because it's not about me, it's about you,
and it's about our family."

00:02:16.091 --> 00:02:22.847
But their courage
still brings me to my knees.

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:24.641
Because no one was talking about it.

00:02:24.683 --> 00:02:27.560
PM: And yet this silent epidemic,

00:02:27.602 --> 00:02:31.189
which it still is in many ways, continues.

00:02:31.189 --> 00:02:33.942
So you and your family took another step.

00:02:33.942 --> 00:02:39.489
You founded an organization
called Bring Change to Mind.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:45.203
GC: I learned about mental health advocacy
at Fountain House in New York,

00:02:45.203 --> 00:02:48.248
and when we decided
that I wanted to do something,

00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:50.417
I wanted to start an organization,

00:02:50.458 --> 00:02:54.087
many generous, wonderful people
who had their own organizations,

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:55.880
the head of Fountain House included,

00:02:55.922 --> 00:02:58.758
sat in that library,
and one of them went online and said,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.719
"What's available? What's available?

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.012
Oh, Bring Change to Mind.
OK, let's use that."

00:03:03.054 --> 00:03:05.014
(Laughter)

00:03:05.056 --> 00:03:07.350
I mean, I didn't even know
I had to have a board.

00:03:07.392 --> 00:03:08.643
(Laughter)

00:03:09.644 --> 00:03:10.895
So --

00:03:10.937 --> 00:03:12.480
(Laughter)

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.941
PM: I think many people
recognize that moment.

00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:16.192
GC: Yeah.

00:03:18.945 --> 00:03:22.449
And that was, oh my gosh,
that was a huge adventure.

00:03:22.449 --> 00:03:26.453
And I thought that my main contribution

00:03:26.453 --> 00:03:29.038
would be making PSAs and things like that.

00:03:29.038 --> 00:03:30.373
We have evolved.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.086
We now have established over --

00:03:35.128 --> 00:03:40.383
it's 540 clubhouses in 43 states.

00:03:40.425 --> 00:03:45.430
(Applause)

00:03:45.472 --> 00:03:50.310
And the thing that I'm very proud of
are the kids, because it's peer-to-peer.

00:03:50.351 --> 00:03:52.896
It's not high schools that write to us.

00:03:52.937 --> 00:03:55.482
It's the kids that write to us.

00:03:55.482 --> 00:03:58.234
The kids say, "We want, we need

00:03:58.234 --> 00:04:01.154
a Bring Change to Mind club
in our high school."

00:04:01.154 --> 00:04:05.575
And I've met a lot of them,
and they are phenomenal.

00:04:05.575 --> 00:04:06.868
(Applause)

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:08.661
PM: And that is transformative.

00:04:08.661 --> 00:04:13.500
GC: Yeah. I think there's
about 14,000 kids now

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:16.920
with, you know, a waiting list always.

00:04:16.961 --> 00:04:21.132
PM: So providing this kind
of peer-to-peer experience,

00:04:21.174 --> 00:04:22.801
especially among teenagers,

00:04:22.842 --> 00:04:26.763
where the suicide rate
and other forms of mental illness

00:04:26.805 --> 00:04:29.015
have been just escalating,

00:04:29.057 --> 00:04:31.017
was a big first step.

00:04:31.059 --> 00:04:32.310
GC: Yeah.

00:04:32.352 --> 00:04:35.021
PM: But you took it beyond that.
You went to Washington.

00:04:35.021 --> 00:04:38.316
You talked publicly
about the lack of resources

00:04:38.316 --> 00:04:42.362
to treat families
suffering with mental illness.

00:04:42.362 --> 00:04:45.865
There were some accomplishments, right?
There have been some successes.

00:04:45.865 --> 00:04:48.117
GC: Yeah. It's actually
very, very exciting.

00:04:48.117 --> 00:04:54.791
And we're at a point where our government
has a bipartisan bill.

00:04:54.833 --> 00:05:00.046
They have put aside 8.5 billion dollars
in the next 10 years

00:05:00.088 --> 00:05:02.048
to establish what we're calling,

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:08.304
what they are calling Certified
Community Behavioral Health Clinics

00:05:08.346 --> 00:05:10.265
in every state of the union --

00:05:10.306 --> 00:05:11.307
(Applause)

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:17.146
And whatever town or county needs them.

00:05:17.146 --> 00:05:23.403
Look into it, because we all
should have it in our counties, at least,

00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:26.447
in our towns, in our cities,

00:05:26.447 --> 00:05:29.701
because actually
we're in a crisis of care.

00:05:29.701 --> 00:05:32.287
PM: And, Glenn,
I know that you were the first

00:05:32.287 --> 00:05:35.039
to always say about this subject,

00:05:35.081 --> 00:05:38.126
"I'm not a mental health expert.
I'm not a doctor.

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:42.463
I came into this
for very personal reasons."

00:05:42.505 --> 00:05:46.342
But I'm sure there are
many people listening to us today

00:05:46.384 --> 00:05:50.805
who are thinking, "Are there signs?
Are there symptoms,

00:05:50.847 --> 00:05:52.932
are there things I should look for?"

00:05:52.974 --> 00:05:57.020
You said yourself you didn't realize
how much Jessie was suffering.

00:05:57.061 --> 00:05:58.313
GC: Oh, not at all.

00:05:58.313 --> 00:06:00.273
PM: What clues, what things did you see?

00:06:00.273 --> 00:06:01.691
GC: First of all, I think --

00:06:01.691 --> 00:06:05.445
My sister Jessie wrote
a book called "Resilience."

00:06:05.445 --> 00:06:09.365
She's so incredibly honest.

00:06:09.365 --> 00:06:14.245
And she talks about her own journey
with bipolar disorder,

00:06:14.245 --> 00:06:18.291
but also her son, when he had
his psychotic break at 19,

00:06:18.333 --> 00:06:20.126
she didn't know what was happening.

00:06:20.168 --> 00:06:22.837
She didn't know what was going on.

00:06:22.879 --> 00:06:26.132
You know, he was thinking
the television was speaking to him.

00:06:26.174 --> 00:06:29.427
He was afraid that the CIA was outside.

00:06:29.469 --> 00:06:30.887
She had no clue.

00:06:30.929 --> 00:06:33.264
And he would, you know, he'd be like this.

00:06:33.306 --> 00:06:37.352
And it was a friend of hers
who had taken psychiatry in college,

00:06:37.393 --> 00:06:41.189
who said, "Jess, let me spend
a little time with him."

00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:44.817
And she said, "I think
he has schizophrenia."

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:47.278
And --

00:06:47.946 --> 00:06:50.031
It was because of that friend.

00:06:50.031 --> 00:06:53.785
I mean, Jessie would have, of course,
if that friend hadn't stepped up,

00:06:53.785 --> 00:06:58.039
I'm sure, you know,
found some answers to his behavior.

00:06:58.081 --> 00:07:01.292
But that's actually --

00:07:01.334 --> 00:07:04.879
I think people hopefully are learning
about that kind of thing.

00:07:05.964 --> 00:07:10.802
But I think it's the anxiety,
it's the depression,

00:07:10.843 --> 00:07:14.681
it's suicidal ideation,

00:07:14.722 --> 00:07:18.393
it's food disorders
that people can so easily hide.

00:07:18.434 --> 00:07:20.311
And the thing you need to be --

00:07:23.147 --> 00:07:26.484
vigilant about is a change in behavior

00:07:26.484 --> 00:07:27.777
that lasts over --

00:07:27.777 --> 00:07:31.239
Like, yes, we all can be depressed
for four or five days,

00:07:31.239 --> 00:07:33.366
the weather changes,
you've lost a boyfriend

00:07:33.366 --> 00:07:38.454
or, you know, God forbid someone has --
you're mourning a death.

00:07:38.454 --> 00:07:41.082
But this is different.

00:07:41.124 --> 00:07:46.254
And when you observe that,
you have to say, "Are you OK?"

00:07:47.588 --> 00:07:50.675
And even if they say yes,

00:07:50.717 --> 00:07:52.927
and you feel differently,

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:55.221
you have to say it again.

00:07:55.263 --> 00:07:57.432
"Are you OK?"

00:07:57.473 --> 00:07:59.392
Until you get --

00:08:00.977 --> 00:08:02.270
what's happening.

00:08:02.311 --> 00:08:04.689
PM: And how are they today? Jessie and --

00:08:04.689 --> 00:08:06.107
GC: Oh, Jessie.

00:08:06.107 --> 00:08:07.650
(Sighs)

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:08.901
Jess --

00:08:09.736 --> 00:08:12.488
I almost hate to use the words,
it's so overused,

00:08:12.488 --> 00:08:14.032
but Jessie is my true hero.

00:08:14.032 --> 00:08:16.325
Both Calen and Jessie.

00:08:16.325 --> 00:08:19.954
I have to tell you, she weeps.

00:08:19.954 --> 00:08:22.623
The meds are hard. She has emphysema.

00:08:22.665 --> 00:08:25.501
She was a wild woman
and smoked a lot of cigarettes.

00:08:25.543 --> 00:08:28.087
(Laughter)

00:08:28.129 --> 00:08:32.050
But she weeps and says,
"I don't want to be this way.

00:08:33.718 --> 00:08:37.055
And the meds, they're going to
shorten my life."

00:08:37.096 --> 00:08:40.308
And she has six grandchildren.

00:08:40.349 --> 00:08:42.393
Now, that's hard.

00:08:42.435 --> 00:08:44.645
It's hard. And --

00:08:45.772 --> 00:08:47.356
Sometimes I say --

00:08:47.356 --> 00:08:50.985
She said to me once,
"I guess I'm the family --"

00:08:52.278 --> 00:08:54.489
What is it, black sheep? No.

00:08:54.489 --> 00:08:56.991
I guess I'm -- it's not
the experimental lamb.

00:08:56.991 --> 00:08:58.993
I forget the word. Anyway.

00:08:58.993 --> 00:09:02.747
Why, out of all of us, was it her?
Why was it Calen?

00:09:03.956 --> 00:09:06.626
We've learned so much.

00:09:08.044 --> 00:09:09.545
But so for --

00:09:09.587 --> 00:09:12.006
I live across the yard from my sister.

00:09:12.048 --> 00:09:15.009
When I'm home,
I'm over there all the time.

00:09:15.051 --> 00:09:19.931
Pippy, my dog,
kind of has a thing going on

00:09:19.972 --> 00:09:21.516
with one of her little dogs.

00:09:21.557 --> 00:09:23.059
(Laughter)

00:09:23.101 --> 00:09:27.355
He won't admit it, but it's obvious.

00:09:27.355 --> 00:09:29.315
(Laughter)

00:09:30.691 --> 00:09:32.151
And I try, you know,

00:09:32.151 --> 00:09:34.237
I try to get her out of the chair.

00:09:34.237 --> 00:09:36.072
You know, "Let's go for a drive."

00:09:36.072 --> 00:09:39.242
You know, "Do you want to go
walk around the block?

00:09:39.242 --> 00:09:40.576
Let's get our other sister

00:09:40.576 --> 00:09:43.329
and, you know, let's just try to get,

00:09:43.329 --> 00:09:45.164
you know, movement and light.

00:09:46.249 --> 00:09:47.583
So it's a constant thing.

00:09:47.625 --> 00:09:49.877
Now, Calen is a beautiful man.

00:09:49.919 --> 00:09:51.921
He's married to -- He's married.

00:09:51.963 --> 00:09:54.674
He's been married
for, I think, 10, 11 years.

00:09:54.715 --> 00:09:57.468
And he's a painter. He's a painter.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:58.761
And --

00:09:59.929 --> 00:10:03.474
He once, early on,
after he came out of the hospital

00:10:03.516 --> 00:10:08.896
and he was brave enough
to go to a local art class,

00:10:08.896 --> 00:10:14.443
he decided that he should tell the teacher
that he was living with schizophrenia.

00:10:14.443 --> 00:10:16.362
So he got up his courage and he said,

00:10:16.362 --> 00:10:19.490
"Sir, I just want you to know
that I'm living with schizophrenia."

00:10:21.742 --> 00:10:23.703
And the teacher said,

00:10:24.745 --> 00:10:25.997
"Don't come back."

00:10:26.539 --> 00:10:27.915
(Audience) Oh...

00:10:29.167 --> 00:10:31.836
He is a fabulous painter.

00:10:31.878 --> 00:10:34.213
(Applause)

00:10:34.255 --> 00:10:37.091
PM: This -- These stories,

00:10:37.133 --> 00:10:41.512
they do remind us
of how big that stigma is still,

00:10:41.554 --> 00:10:45.141
as one of the biggest barriers
to people getting the help.

00:10:45.183 --> 00:10:48.352
And Glen, there's just
such gratitude that we all feel

00:10:48.394 --> 00:10:51.689
for the courage that you had, and your --

00:10:51.689 --> 00:10:57.236
I know, but you fought for your sister
and for your nephew, and for all.

00:10:57.236 --> 00:10:59.197
GC: Yeah. Now we've got to scale it out.

00:10:59.197 --> 00:11:02.408
We have to scale out this community
health care system we have.

00:11:02.408 --> 00:11:04.785
It's a crisis of care.

00:11:04.785 --> 00:11:08.164
You know, the treatments are there.

00:11:08.206 --> 00:11:10.875
And they work, and people can recover.

00:11:10.917 --> 00:11:14.337
But you can't just treat people
when they have a break,

00:11:14.378 --> 00:11:16.714
and you give them a couple of meds.

00:11:16.756 --> 00:11:19.300
You've got to be in it for the long haul.

00:11:19.342 --> 00:11:23.179
And we have to have structures like that
across our country.

00:11:23.221 --> 00:11:25.431
PM: And because of your work,
we will have more

00:11:25.473 --> 00:11:28.267
and because of the work
of Bring Change to Mind and --

00:11:28.309 --> 00:11:30.478
(Applause)

00:11:37.985 --> 00:11:39.278
(Audience) Aww!

00:11:39.278 --> 00:11:40.571
GC: Pippy!

00:11:40.571 --> 00:11:41.822
(Laughter)

00:11:42.907 --> 00:11:45.785
Here you are! Here you are!

00:11:48.788 --> 00:11:52.500
PM: This is Mr. Pip, everyone.

00:11:54.126 --> 00:11:55.878
(Laughter)

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:57.171
GC: Pippy!

00:11:57.213 --> 00:12:00.383
PM: I think this is
your mental illness companion.

00:12:00.424 --> 00:12:01.842
GC: It is.

00:12:01.884 --> 00:12:04.804
Do you have anything to say?
Do you want to speak?

00:12:04.845 --> 00:12:06.097
Good job!

00:12:06.138 --> 00:12:07.932
PM: Thank you, everyone.

00:12:10.017 --> 00:12:11.269
GC: Thank you.

